this episode of real engineering is  brought to you by hover get 10% off your  next domain purchase using the code real  engineering in 2007 Steve Jobs  introduced the original iPhone one of  the most innovative and revolutionary  products of the 21st century assuring in  a new era of smart phones and waiting in  line to get one the furor over this new  phone was due in large part to the LCD  multi-touch panel and that allowed you  to see and interact with the device with  a clarity and mobility never seen before  Apple wasn't the first to introduce  touch panel LCDs into phones but they  were the first to make people care about  it  they revolutionized what was once a  clunky and frustrating technology - one  that could be easily used for typing on  screen and gesture control the screen  itself featured a 3.5 inch diagonal 320  by 480 resolution LCD major shifts and  iPhone usability and design over the  years have been around the display from  the iPhone to the iPhone 3GS the phones  had a display density of 163 pixels per  inch the iPhone 4 Retina display double  dot the 326 PPI this pixel size remained  constant for another seven years while  generational progress was made in LCD  technology more consistent backlighting  consistent color and brightness when  viewing off angle and a wider color  gamut gave the new iPhone 8 the gold  standard LCD panel but for the iPhone 10  Apple has taken a sudden departure from  its fateful LCD screens and jumping in  bed with her archrival Samsung  who are manufacturing its new OLED  display the Apple watch used OLED but  Samsung has been utilizing a maturing  OLED technology for years in their  Galaxy smartphones this is the first  time most Apple enthusiasts will  experience OLED technology so what are  the fundamental differences between LCD  and OLED lcd stands for a liquid crystal  display the liquid crystals used falls  somewhere between a liquid and a solid  which allows them to change their  molecular orientation when an electrical  current is applied this is incredibly  useful as it allows us to use the  molecules to manipulate light that is  passing through the liquid crystal  liquid crystal pixels do not produce  their own light but in modern LCD panels  LED backlighting is used with a  backlight uniformity filter added to  ensure even lighting across the panel  next the light passes through a  polarization film which only lets light  traveling in the horizontal orientation  to pass  through followed by the liquid crystal  group and then another polarizing filter  orientated 90 degrees to the first if  you're familiar with polarizing filters  you will know that light cannot pass  through two filters when they're aligned  perpendicular to each other so light  depends on this liquid crystal layer to  orientate the light to allow to pass  through the second filter the liquid  crystal layer is sandwiched between two  electrodes and two pieces of H glass  forcing the liquid crystal molecules  into a near predictable 90 degree twist  when a voltage is applied through the  electrodes the crystal molecules begin  to realign in the direction of the  current a larger voltage will result in  a full 90 degree twist as light passes  through it will follow the direction of  the crystal molecules before  encountering the second polarization  filter so the liquid crystal layer  essentially acts as a controllable light  valve if light is allowed to pass it  will travel through a colored filter  giving it one of three colors red blue  or green this structure is called a  sub-pixel  combine three subpixels with red green  and blue filters and you have a single  traditional RGB liquid crystal pixel  this brings up a few really interesting  characteristics of LCDs the first is  that even when the panel is displaying a  black image the backlight is always on  causing a large battery drain and some  light leakage at pixel boundaries and at  the edges of the screen which negatively  affects the screen contrast ratio with  the iPhone X Apple engineers have  adopted a fundamentally new display  technology OLED or organic  light-emitting diodes Samsung have a  near perfect monopoly on the  manufacturing market for these screens  manufacturing 90% of the world's supply  and holding the majority of the world's  patents forcing competitors like Apple  to purchase from them to remain up to  date with the best technology on the  market although it hasn't stopped them  from reportedly investing billions in  Samsung's major competitor LG display  OLED works very differently from LCD in  a few key ways for starters there's no  backlight instead each pixel is  self-illuminating through the light  emitting process called electrophoresis  there are two organic compounds called  the emissive and conductive layers that  are sandwiched between an anode and a  cathode as an electrical current flows  from the cathode to the anode electrons  are given to the emissive layer and  removed from the conductive layer the  electrons in the emissive layer now  migrate to fill the holes in the  conductive layer  when they do extra energy is released as  a photon this entire process is  controlled and structured by the TFT  matrix and the entire structure is  placed on a substrate of either glass or  polymer since there is no backlight and  each individual pixel is individually  controlled substantial power saving can  occur when displaying dark or black  images in addition OLED panels are  significantly thinner and lighter than  their LCD counterparts allowing for  slimmer devices with razor-sharp bezels  and flexible screens like the one in the  Samsung Galaxy s8 lastly OLED panels of  high brightness and true zero pixel  values leading to insane contrast ratios  of over 1 million to one  blacks are inky colors are more vibrant  and localized brightness can be greatly  improved there are some growing pains  with OLED technology holding it back in  the mainstream consumer market  the chief limiting factor is burnin  burnin occurs when the organic layers of  the pixel begin to break down from  prolonged usage as the layers decay they  become significantly less bright than  the pixels around them which can make  stationary UI elements appear as ghost  images this mainly occurs from prolonged  static images such as user interface  icons and as the single largest reason  oled computer monitors or TVs are not  yet mainstream not to mention the cost  which can be 1.5 to 2 times higher than  an equivalent LCD panel Oh lad has  fundamental strengths over LCD that will  open up possibilities for how and where  displays are used in the world around us  LG Display has a consumer OLED  television that is only 2.5 7  millimeters thick they are working on  transparent ohlet's that can be  integrated into glass panels and various  OLED manufacturers are working towards  foldable displays with continued  improvement OLED displays look set to  revolutionize the way we consume and  view information in the world around us  websites on your phone will look better  than ever but what's a good website  without a great domain name hover is the  easiest and best place to register a  custom domain or email you can even  purchase domains right from your iPhone  using Apple pay they have over 400  domain extensions and the best customer  service in the business seriously  they've won awards I couldn't believe my  luck when I saw that the real  engineering domain was available on  hover and purchased it immediately and  it took less than 20 seconds to make it  mine I now have a custom email address  that couldn't suit my channel better if  you're looking for a unique domain name  hover  the best place to get one and you can  get 10% off your next purchase using the  code real engineering you could get the  real investments domain and use it for  your online investment training site or  you could get the real tough security  domain name for your cyber security  block whatever do